// `try testing.check(...)` in a test body — a `try` whose propagated payload is
// itself an AssertionFailure.
//
// Run under `nomi test` and as a compiled binary, the two
// they DISAGREED. The interpreter reports the check's own failure:
//
//     line N: check failed
//       1 == 2
//     values:
//       ...
//
// The compiled binary reported the generic early exit instead:
//
//     line N: test returned early
//       try testing.check(1 == 2)
//       returned:
//         Err(AssertionFailure{...})
//
// So the same source produced a DIFFERENT DIAGNOSIS on the two paths — not a
// formatting difference, a different answer about what went wrong. A reader of
// the compiled report is told the test exited early and handed a struct dump;
// the interpreter tells them which assertion failed and what the values were.
//
// The rule the interpreter states, and the one that had no counterpart here:
// `EarlyReturnFailureFromError` DECLINES when the propagated value is an
// AssertionFailure (eval.go), so the assertion arm wins at all three of its
// call sites. `try` carrying an assertion failure is an ASSERTION failure that
// happens to have travelled through a `try`; the `try` is transport, not the
// diagnosis.
//
// Four cases, and the fourth is the one that catches an over-broad fix:
//
//   - A passing `try testing.check` unwraps and the case runs on.
//   - A failing one reports the CHECK's failure, with its own line and values,
//     and NOT `test returned early`.
//   - A failing check over a SHAPE subject (a Result) does the same. This is
//     reachable only since `CheckJudged` landed; before that a shape subject was
//     refused outright, so the wider input set is newer than the bug.
//   - An ordinary `try` over a plain `Err` in the SAME FILE still reports
//     `test returned early`. A fix that routed every test-body `try` through
//     the assertion arm would break this row, and it would break it silently
//     on the compiled side only — which is exactly the shape of the bug being
//     fixed, inverted.
import {
    std/io
    std/testing
}

fn parse(n: Int): Result<Int, String> {
    if n < 0 {
        Err("negative ${n}")
    } else {
        Ok(n * 2)
    }
}

fn shape(n: Int): Result<Int, String> {
    if n < 0 { Err("bad ${n}") } else { Ok(n) }
}

test "a passing check unwraps and the case runs on" {
    passed = try testing.check(1 == 1)
    io.print("after the passing check")

    assert passed
}

test "a failing check reports the check and not an early return" {
    io.print("before the failing check")
    passed = try testing.check(1 == 2)

    io.print("this line must not run")

    assert passed
}

test "a failing check over a shape subject reports the check" {
    v = try testing.check(shape(-3))

    io.print("this line must not run either")

    assert v == Ok(3)
}

test "an ordinary try still reports test returned early" {
    v = try parse(-1)

    assert v == 0
}

test "the case after a propagating one still runs" {
    assert 1 == 1
}
